 See also [cardinality explorer docs](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/victoriametrics/single-server-victoriametrics/#cardinality-explorer).
 
+## Limiting number of targets per job
+
+By default, `vmagent` scrapes all the targets discovered for a given `job_name`, regardless of how many
+targets were discovered and which [service discovery](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/victoriametrics/sd_configs/)
+mechanism(s) provided them. This is usually the desired behavior, but a misconfiguration on the service
+discovery side (for example, an accidentally removed tag filter or an overly broad catalog query) may
+suddenly cause a single job to discover a huge number of targets. Scraping all of them at once can spike
+memory and CPU usage on the `vmagent` instance and degrade scraping for every other job running on it.
+
+An optional safety limit on the number of targets scraped per job can be enforced in the following places:
+
+* Via `-promscrape.maxScrapeTargetsPerJob` command-line flag. This limit is applied by default
+  to all the `scrape_config` entries defined in the file pointed to by `-promscrape.config`.
+* Via `max_scrape_targets` config option at [scrape_config](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/victoriametrics/sd_configs/#scrape_configs) section.
+  The `max_scrape_targets` allows overriding the `-promscrape.maxScrapeTargetsPerJob` on a per-`scrape_config` basis.
+
+By default, i.e. when neither `-promscrape.maxScrapeTargetsPerJob` nor `max_scrape_targets` is set, the number
+of targets per job is unlimited, so existing setups are unaffected.
+
+If the number of discovered targets for a job exceeds the configured limit, then `vmagent` keeps scraping
+only the first `max_scrape_targets` of them, while the rest are dropped instead of being scraped. This limit
+is applied uniformly regardless of which service discovery mechanism(s) supplied the targets for the job -
+`vmagent` counts all the currently scraped targets for the `job_name`, no matter whether they come from
+`kubernetes_sd_configs`, `consul_sd_configs`, `static_configs` or any other combination of
+[supported service discovery mechanisms](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/victoriametrics/sd_configs/#supported-service-discovery-configs).
+
+Targets dropped because of this limit show up at the [`/targets` and `/service-discovery` pages](#monitoring)
+and at the `/api/v1/targets` API the same way as targets dropped by [relabeling](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/victoriametrics/relabeling/),
+with the `max scrape targets exceeded` drop reason. This makes it easy to spot which job(s) are currently
+hitting the limit and roughly how many targets are being excluded because of it, using the same places
+operators already use to check on scrape target health.
+
 ## Monitoring
 
 `vmagent` exports various metrics in Prometheus exposition format at `http://vmagent-host:8429/metrics` page.
